On Jul 16, 7:16 am, Topi Linkala <n...@iki.fi> wrote:

> There is a problem on having same measure between
> ascended games and those that end earlier.

Agreed, and I considered discussing that, but the
"Starting Score Factors Lists" posting was getting
long enough already.

> Would you calculate a true pacifist or fast
> ascension highscore for a game where the player is
> killed before the first move?

Well, it wouldn't be an ascension, but you make the
good point that scoring for stuff like conducts only
makes sense in terms of ascensions.

> So we might end up with two different high score
> tables: one for ascensions and an other for
> non-ascensions.

Yes, and for situations like NAO, having one score
table for non-ascensions, with either a large but
limited number of entries, or probably better,
entries that AGE OFF THE TABLE so that it doesn't
suffer as the current one does that the lowest score
is pretty much unachievable for most players, would
be nice, so that non-ascending players would have
some table of "RECENT Best Non-Ascending Scores"
against which to compete and gauge their progress.

That would nerf the annoying MAXINT-1 entries, which
would simply age off the table with time.

For ascensions, I think it would be better if there
were a separate "demigods table" which should
include _all_ ascensions ever on a site, since
having your ascension score kicked off the existing
table for being a low score when score at all as
currently implemented is pretty generally agreed to
be meaningless for ascensions, looks spectacularly
unfair.

> Or measuring different things on ascencion vs.
> non-ascension.

That, too, and To Be Designed.

> I personally prefer separate lists.

I agree, as noted above. I'm guessing that separate
lists would still be a good idea even with a revised
scoring system.

